From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: PatchForge
Date: Sat, 6 Sep 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Robustness: skip tags missing from AST structure

tree-sitter extracts def/ref names that the ast-based structure may
lack (e.g. vendored Python-2 files such as requests' chardet fail
ast.parse on Python 3, yielding empty class/function lists).
The unguarded lookups structure_classes[tag_name] /
structure_all_funcs[tag_name] then raise KeyError and abort the whole
graph build. Observed on psf/requests @ a0df2cb:
KeyError: 'CharDistributionAnalysis'.

Fix: skip def-tags with no structure entry (no line info available
for them anyway). Ref-tags never touch the structure and are
unaffected. Behavior on well-formed repos is unchanged.

Apply from the RepoGraph clone root (after repograph-windows-paths.patch):
  git apply <workspace>/patches/repograph-missing-tag-guard.patch

Revert:
  git -C third_party/RepoGraph checkout -- repograph/construct_graph.py
  (then re-apply repograph-windows-paths.patch if still needed)

Upstream status: not fixed upstream as of 2026-09-06.
Do NOT commit into third_party/; this file is the record (§24).

--- a/repograph/construct_graph.py
+++ b/repograph/construct_graph.py
@@ -330,6 +330,10 @@
                 continue
 
             if category == 'class':
+                if tag_name not in structure_classes:
+                    # ast.parse failed for this file (e.g. py2 vendored code);
+                    # no line info available, skip the tag.
+                    continue
                 # try:
                 #     class_functions = self.get_class_functions(tree_ast, tag_name)
                 # except:
@@ -352,6 +355,10 @@
             elif category == 'function':
 
                 if kind == 'def':
+                    if tag_name not in structure_all_funcs:
+                        # ast.parse failed for this file; no line info
+                        # available, skip the tag.
+                        continue
                     # func_block = self.get_func_block(cur_cdl, code)
                     # cur_cdl =func_block
                     cur_cdl = '\n'.join(structure_all_funcs[tag_name]['text'])
